  "Question": "A \\(2\\)-digit number is such that the product of the digits plus the sum of the digits is equal to the number. What is the units digit of the number?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( 1 \\)",
    "B": "\\( 3 \\)",
    "C": "\\( 5 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 7 \\)",
    "E": "\\( 9 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Let \\(a\\) be the tens digit and \\(b\\) the units digit. Since the number has two digits, \\(1\\leq a\\leq9\\) and \\(0\\leq b\\leq9\\). Its value is \\(10a+b\\), so the given condition becomes\n\\[\nab+a+b=10a+b.\n\\]\nSubtract \\(b\\) and then \\(a\\) from both sides to isolate the product of the digits:\n\\[\nab=9a.\n\\]\nThe tens digit \\(a\\) is not zero, so dividing both sides by \\(a\\) gives \\(b=9\\).\n\nThis value works: for any allowed tens digit \\(a\\),\n\\[\na\\cdot9+a+9=10a+9.\n\\]\nThus the units digit is \\(9\\), choice E.",
